5. UNLICENSED COMMERCIAL USE
================================================================================

5.1  If You engage in Commercial Use without a Commercial License, every license
     granted to You by this License terminates automatically on the first day of
     that use, and You owe the Licensor the Unlicensed Use Fee.

5.2  The Unlicensed Use Fee is the greater of:
     (a) three (3) times the amount that would have been payable under the
         Commercial Terms for the period of unlicensed use, without any free
         tier, discount or waiver, and
     (b) a reasonable royalty for that use, as determined under Section 5.4.
     You also owe the Licensor's costs of investigating and enforcing, including
     reasonable attorneys' fees and expert costs. The Licensor may instead elect
     to recover Your profits attributable to the unlicensed use, or actual and
     statutory damages where the law provides them.

5.3  The parties agree that actual damages from unlicensed Commercial Use are
     difficult to measure, that the Unlicensed Use Fee is a reasonable
     pre-estimate of them and an incentive to use the published Commercial Terms
     instead of unlicensed use, and that it is not a penalty. The Licensor may
     also obtain injunctive relief to stop unlicensed Commercial Use, and the
     parties agree that such use causes harm for which money alone is not an
     adequate remedy.

5.4  If a court or arbitral tribunal finds any part of this Section
     unenforceable, that part is reduced to the largest amount or measure the
     tribunal finds enforceable, and the Licensor may elect the remedies in the
     last sentence of Section 5.2 instead. A reasonable royalty is measured by
     the Commercial Terms as a minimum benchmark, because they are the price at
     which the Licensor offers the same rights to everyone.

5.5  Regularizing. If You stop the unlicensed use and promptly disclose it, the
     Licensor may, at its sole discretion, reinstate Your license on payment of
     the amounts that would have been due under the Commercial Terms plus
     interest. Nothing obliges the Licensor to do so.

